  "answer_text": "Hipparchus of Nicaea (c. 190 \u2013 c. 120 BC) was an ancient Greek astronomer, geographer, and mathematician widely regarded as the greatest astronomical observer of antiquity. While he is highly famous for his astronomical *discoveries* (such as the precession of the equinoxes), he is also credited with inventing several foundational mathematical systems, coordinates, and physical instruments:\n\n### 1. Trigonometry (The Table of Chords)\nHipparchus is widely recognized as the **founder and inventor of trigonometry**. To solve complex astronomical problems, he developed the first known **trigonometric table (a table of chords)**, which is the direct ancestor of the modern sine table. This allowed astronomers to calculate the properties of triangles mathematically rather than relying purely on geometric drawings.\n\n### 2. Terrestrial Latitude and Longitude\nWhile earlier scholars had contemplated the Earth's sphericity, Hipparchus was the first to invent a systematic coordinate grid of **latitude and longitude** to pinpoint locations on Earth. He transferred the concept of celestial coordinates down to the Earth and proposed that local latitude and longitude should be determined using astronomical observations (such as timing lunar eclipses). He was also the first to divide the Earth's grid lines into $360^\\circ$.\n\n### 3. The Stellar Magnitude Scale\nWhen compiling his star catalog\u2014the first comprehensive star catalog of the Western world, containing about 850 stars\u2014Hipparchus invented the **magnitude scale** to rank the brightness of stars. He classified the brightest stars as \"first magnitude,\" the next brightest as \"second magnitude,\" down to the faintest visible stars at \"sixth magnitude\". A highly refined version of this system is still used by modern astronomers today.\n\n### 4. The Planispheric Astrolabe\nHipparchus is heavily credited by historians with inventing (or at least being the first to describe and construct) the **planispheric astrolabe**. By mathematically projecting the 3D celestial sphere onto a 2D plane (stereographic projection), he created a portable, hand-held analog device that astronomers and sailors used for centuries to calculate the time and find the positions of stars and the Sun.\n\n### 5. Astronomical Instruments\n* **Hipparchus's Dioptra:** He invented a modified version of the *dioptra* (a sighting tube) specifically designed for astronomy. It featured a sliding mechanism used to measure the precise apparent angular diameters of the Sun and the Moon.\n* **The Armillary Sphere:** Though the invention of the armillary sphere is sometimes disputed, Hipparchus is widely credited with either inventing or significantly refining it into a highly functional physical model of the celestial coordinates to assist in his observation and mapping of the night sky.\n\n### 6. The Antikythera Mechanism (Disputed)\nBecause the **Antikythera Mechanism**\u2014the world's oldest known analog computer\u2014uses gear systems that perfectly match Hipparchus's complex mathematical theories of the Moon\u2019s variable motion, some modern scholars believe he may have been the mastermind behind its design or the intellectual grandfather of the device.",
